dency→dependent convention. Rule 11 is satisfied — the docs-authoring epic (E005) is blocked by all four Implementation epics. E001→E003 and E002→E003 are justified Rule 12/13 build-against edges (comparison surface; registry linkage API), not artificial serialization; E004 is correctly parallel to E001/E002/E003 (different repo, no shared artifact, coordination risk only — the summary says so explicitly). No konflux-onboarding, license-validation, or repo-onboarding epics exist, so Rules 7/8/10 parallel-edge checks are moot; no build-pipeline-output-as-content edges exist. Critical path length 3 (E001/E002 → E003 → E005) matches the declared `critical_path_length: 3` and is structurally forced by the Rule 13 registry-API chain plus the mandatory Rule 11 docs epic — reasonable for a 3-component strategy with a cross-team registry integration. Cross-check of epic content found no data/artifact dependency missing from the DAG: E003's consumption of the registry linkage API is edged to E002, and E001 explicitly introduces no new backend data plane.\n25\t\n26\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n27\tEach component/team tuple produces its own epic: Dashboard/RHOAI Dashboard (E001, E003), Model Registry/Model Registry (E002), Data Science Pipelines/RHOAI Pipelines (E004), Documentation/Documentation (E005). No epic spans multiple components or teams. No scope duplication: E003 explicitly extends and reuses the E001 comparison surface (\"do not build a second comparison surface\"; AC \"reuses the existing run comparison view rather than duplicating it\") rather than rebuilding it — the boundary between E001 and E003 is the registry-entry-point increment, drawn along the dependency on E002's API, which is the right axis. The E001-AC3/E004 overlap is incidental, not duplication: E001's AC is on its own deliverable (the view renders DSP runs), while E004 is the owning team's validation of the same existing surfaces per the passive-component exception, as the Health Warnings explain